#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 4.6.26
# DESCRIPTION
# Given a position function s(t), find:
#    (a) The velocity and acceleration functions
#    (b) When velocity is positive/negative
#    (c) When acceleration is positive/negative
#    (d) The times when the particle is speeding up/slowing down

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Rates of change - engineering and physics)
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('4.6')
## Problem1('18')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Applied Problems', 'Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Speed', 'Position')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',      'PGML.pl',
    'contextFraction.pl', 
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

#    s(t) = (t^2 - A)^2
Context("Numeric")->variables->add(t => 'Real');
$Atmp = random(1, 6, 1);
$A    = 3 * $Atmp;
$B    = random(3, 7, 2);

$Posfunc = Formula("$A t^2 - $A*$B t")->reduce;

#   (a) Velocity & Acceleration functions
$Velfunc = Formula("2*$A t - $A*$B")->reduce;
$Accfunc = Formula("2*$A")->reduce;

#   (b,c) When is velocity positive? negative?
Context("Fraction");
$Endpt = Formula("$B/2")->reduce;
Context("Interval");
$Velposint = Interval("($Endpt,inf)");
$Velnegint = Interval("[0,$Endpt)");

#   (d) Times when particle is speeding up/slowing down
#       -  Velocity and acceleration must be same sign
$Speedup  = $Velposint;
$Slowdown = $Velnegint;

BEGIN_PGML
The function [` s(t) `] describes the position of a particle moving along a coordinate line,
where [`s`] is in feet and [`t`] is in seconds.
[``` s(t) = [$Posfunc], \qquad t \ge 0 ```]

a) Find the velocity and acceleration functions.

    [# [. [`v(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Velfunc} .]*
     [. [`a(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Accfunc} .]* 
     #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

b) Over what interval is the particle moving in the positive direction?

    [_]{$Velposint}

c) Over what interval is the particle moving in the negative direction?

    [_]{$Velnegint}

d) Over what interval is the particle speeding up? Slowing down?

    [# [. Speeding Up:   .] [. [_]{$Speedup} .]*
      [.  Slowing Down: .] [. [_]{$Slowdown} .]*
    #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) To find the velocity function, take the derivative of the position function, [` s(t) `]. 
[``` s\;'(t) = v(t) = [$Velfunc]. ```]
    To find the acceleration function, take the derivative of the velocity function.
[``` v\;'(t) = a(t) = [$Accfunc]. ```]
    Note that this is also the second derivative of the position function.

b) The particle will be moving in the positive direction when its velocity is greater than 0.
[```[$Velfunc] > 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$A] (2t - [$B]) > 0 \quad \Rightarrow t > [$Endpt] ```]
This is the interval [`\left([$Endpt],\infty\right)`].

b) The particle will be moving in the negative direction when its velocity is less than 0.
    [```[$Velfunc] < 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$A] (2t - [$B]) < 0 \quad \Rightarrow t < [$Endpt] ```]
    Since [`t \ge 0`], this is the interval [`\left[0,[$Endpt]\right)`].

d) To determine when the particle is speeding up or slowing down, we need 
    to look at the signs of the velocity and acceleration:

    - If the velocity is positive, then a positive acceleration 
will cause it to speed up
    - If the velocity is negative, then a negative acceleration
will cause it to speed up

Therefore, the particle is speeding up whenever the velocity and acceleration are of the same sign. Note that the acceleration is a fixed positive number, so the particle is speeding up when the velocity is positive and is slowing down when the velocity is negative. So the particle is speeding up over [`\left([$Endpt],\infty\right)`] and slowing down over [`\left[0,[$Endpt]\right)`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
